+**Edge Cases:**
+- **No Lights:** Return zero or background [S2, unofficial].
+- **Self-Intersection:** Shadow ray origin should be offset slightly along normal to avoid self-intersection [G].
+- **Precision:** Floating point errors in $t_l \le t_{min}$ comparison can cause flickering; use a small epsilon [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Light Sampling for NEE](2616-light-sampling-for-nee.md)
-Estimates direct illumination in participating media by explicitly sampling light sources and their surfaces to generate connection points for path tracing. It differs from [Shadow Ray Casting](2796-shadow-ray-casting.md) by using probabilistic sampling with importance weighting, and from this method by explicitly computing geometry and PDFs for unbiased Nearest Energy Estimation. Choose it when unbiased direct lighting in participating media is required.
+Reduces variance in direct illumination by explicitly sampling light sources, decoupling light selection from path vertex generation.
 
 ### [Direct Illumination Sampling with Mesh Intersection](4450-direct-illumination-sampling-with-mesh-intersection.md)
-Computes direct illumination for a surface point by sampling a light source and verifying visibility against explicit triangle meshes. It differs from SDF-based approaches by relying on geometric primitives rather than implicit distance fields. Choose it for polygonal scenes.
+Handles direct illumination for polygonal scenes using explicit triangle meshes, distinguishing itself from SDF-based approaches.
 
 ### [Light Ray Sampling](4885-light-ray-sampling.md)
-Computes direct illumination at a volume sample point by stepping along the direction to a distant light source, accumulating optical density, and applying a phase function. It differs from mesh-based shadow casting by explicitly integrating density through the participating medium, allowing for soft, volumetric shadows. Choose it for volumetric soft shadows.
+Computes direct illumination by stepping along the direction to a distant light, integrating density for soft volumetric shadows.
 
 ### [Direct Light Contribution Estimation](5411-direct-light-contribution-estimation.md)
-Computes the intensity of light arriving directly from a specific light source at a point in a participating medium, accounting for attenuation by both local geometry and global scene occlusion. It differs from its siblings by explicitly separating the attenuation calculation into a high-resolution local segment and a lower-resolution global segment. Choose it when fine local occlusion details are critical.
+Separates attenuation into high-resolution local and low-resolution global segments for accurate first-order scattering.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{\text{objects}})$ per sample when testing one selected light against all scene objects [S1, S2, unofficial]. If testing all $N_{\text{lights}}$ lights, complexity scales to $O(N_{\text{lights}} \cdot N_{\text{objects}})$ per pixel [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory, excluding scene graph storage [G].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; provides sharp, correct shadows for point and directional lights; efficient for scenes with few dominant lights [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Can be noisy for complex lighting environments with many small lights, requiring many samples; evaluates only direct paths and does not compute indirect bounces [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Real-time rendering with simple lighting; offline rendering where direct lighting is the primary concern; scenes with few light sources [G].
-- **When not to use:** Scenes requiring soft shadows from large area lights without multiple samples; scenes dominated by indirect lighting where path tracing or photon mapping is preferred [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot L)$ per pixel, where $N$ is the number of bounces and $L$ is the number of lights (if testing all) or $O(N)$ if using a sampler to select one light [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space per ray, excluding scene data structures [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Limited by hardware constraints; launching infinite rays uniformly is not feasible for real-time FPS (>15 FPS) [S1, unofficial]. Post-processing filters (Gaussian, bilinear) are often applied to reduce noise [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Accurate shadowing and direct illumination; simple to implement [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** High variance (noise) in complex scenes; requires many samples for convergence [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method is used in Sparse Voxel Octree compression and global illumination studies, where it is referred to as "Iluminación directa" [S1, unofficial]. It is also implemented in educational path tracing repositories to handle direct illumination sampling for various light types [S2, unofficial].
+Originated in early ray tracing literature for visualizing shadows and light contributions in participating media and scenes [S1, unofficial]. The method is described in the context of Sparse Voxel Octree compression and efficiency studies, where direct illumination is added to improve visual quality by shooting rays from intersection points to light sources [S1, unofficial]. It is also implemented in educational path tracers for handling different light types and ensuring visibility [S2, unofficial].
 
